# The canonical session/continuity domain of the Rust executable: the files
# scripts/session-coverage-threshold is measured over. One repository-relative path per
# line; blank lines and comments are ignored.
#
# WHY THIS IS ONE FILE TODAY, AND WHAT WAS CONSIDERED FOR IT.
#
# `capability/builtin/continuity.rs` is the whole of the session and continuity domain in
# this executable, and that is a fact about where the domain lives rather than a choice made
# here. The module says so in its own first paragraph — "It is read, never written. The
# lifecycle is the shell tool's" — and ADR 0052 names that split as the structural cause of
# the outage it was written after: the shell owns the writes and the state machine, this
# crate owns the typed read model, and nothing spans both. The single canonical domain
# service ADR 0052 settles on does not exist yet; it is staged work. When it lands, its files
# belong on this list and the threshold follows them.
#
# Considered and left out, each for a reason:
#
#   src/policy.rs             holds no session key on this branch. `session.freshness` is
#                             added by the ADR 0052 branch and is not here; adding the file
#                             now would put four hundred lines of projection loading into a
#                             session threshold and make the number say less, not more.
#   builtin/obligations.rs    reads the ledger and shares `read_task` with continuity, but
#                             what it models is a task's delivery obligations (ADR 0030),
#                             not the episode lifecycle.
#   builtin/health.rs         reports on the stores among many other things; the session
#                             half of it is a handful of lines inside a different subject.
#   devtask/task.rs           carries session records as evidence for an issue. The relation
#                             is to the issue, not to the episode.
#
# The shell half of the lifecycle — lib/session.sh, capture.sh, checkpoint.sh, handover.sh,
# derive.sh, common.sh, session_context.sh — is not measured by cargo-llvm-cov and is not on
# this list. `scripts/shell-coverage` measures it instead, exactly for functions and as a
# stated floor for lines, and reports rather than gates.
apps/majordomus-cli/src/capability/builtin/continuity.rs
